264:  The constrained quadratic optimization routine is based on an algorithm that uses the interior point method
265:  to solve the constrained quadratic programming problem (see Vanderbei 1999 for more details on the interior point method).
266: It is implemented via a C++ plugin and has the following 
267: tuning parameters:{p_end}
268: 
269: {p 8 8 2}
270: {opt "margin(real)"} Margin for constraint violation tolerance. Default is 5 percent (ie. 0.05).{p_end}
271: {p 8 8 2}
272: {opt "maxiter(#)"} Maximum number of iterations. Default is 1000.{p_end}
273: {p 8 8 2}
274: {opt "sigf(#)"} Precision (no of significant figures). Default is 7.{p_end}
275: {p 8 8 2}
276: {opt "bound(#)"} Clipping bound for the variables. Default is 10.{p_end}
277: 
278: {p 4 4 2}
279: 
280: Additional control parameters for the {it: nested optimization routine}:
281: 
282: {p 5 5 2}
283: If {cmd: nested} is specified, a nested optimization will be performed using the 
284: constrained quadratic programming routine and Stata's {help ml} optimizer. By default, {cmd: synth}
285:  uses the {help maximize} default settings. The user 
286: may tune the {help maximize} settings depending on his application (e.g. like
287: synth ... , iterate(20) ).{p_end}
288: {synoptline}
289: 
290: {title:Saved Results}
291: 
292: {p 4 8 2}
293: By default, {cmd:synth} ereturns the following matrices, which 
294: can be displayed by typing {cmd: ereturn list} after 
295: {cmd: synth} is finished (also see {help ereturn}).  
296: 
297: {p 8 8 2}
298: {cmd: e(V_matrix):}{p_end}
299: {p 10 10 2}
300: A diagonal matrix that contains the normalized variable weights in the diagonal.
301: 
302: {p 8 8 2}
303: {cmd: e(X_balance) :}{p_end}
304: {p 10 10 2}
305: A matrix that juxtaposes the predictor values for the unit affected by the intervention
306: and the synthetic control unit. The matrix has two columns (treated and synthetic) and as many rows as predictors. 
307: 
308: {p 8 8 2}
309: {cmd: e(W_weights) :}{p_end}
310: {p 10 10 2}
311: A matrix that contains the unit numbers and unit weights, ie. the relative contribution of each control unit to the synthetic control unit.
312: The matrix has two column and as many rows as control units.
313: 
314: {p 8 8 2}
315: {cmd: e(Y_treated) :}{p_end}
316: {p 10 10 2}
317: A matrix that contains the values of the response variable for the treated unit for each time period.
318: The matrix has one column and as many rows as time periods specified in {cmd:resultsperiod()}. 
319: 
320: {p 8 8 2}
321: {cmd: e(Y_synthetic) :}{p_end}
322: {p 10 10 2}
323: A matrix that contains the values of the response variable for the synthetic control unit 
324: for each time period.
325: The matrix has one column and as many rows as time periods specified in {cmd:resultsperiod(). 
326: 
